sentences.t:
et si continuo victorem ea cura subisset,
rumpere claustra manu sociosque immittere portis,
ultimus ille dies bello gentique fuisset;

sentences.l:
and if at once the victor that thought had come over,
to burst the bars with his hand and his comrades to let in at the gates,
the last would that day for the war and for the nation have been;

he adds Halys, the comrade, and, his shield pierced through, Phegeus;
then, unaware, on the walls and stirring up the War-god,
Alcander and Halius and Noemon and Prytanis.

sentences.t:
Lyncea tendentem contra sociosque vocantem
vibranti gladio conixus ab aggere dexter
occupat;

sentences.l:
Lynceus, making towards him and his comrades calling,
with flashing sword, straining from the rampart, on the right,
he forestalls;

sentences.t:
Tum toto corpore sudor
liquitur et piceum, nec respirare potestas,
flumen agit;

sentences.l:
Then over his whole body sweat
melts, and a pitch-black — nor is there power to take breath —
stream it drives;
